# Scripts and prose: keep them LF so the node/python sources hash the same on
# every platform.
#
# ORDER MATTERS HERE. git applies the LAST matching line, so the vendored-tree
# rules have to come AFTER the text rules: `*.js text eol=lf` would otherwise
# re-assert `text` on pyodide.js and pyodide.asm.js after `binary` had cleared
# it, and the file would be marked text while the comment above claimed it was
# binary. `git check-attr -a assets/vendor/pyodide/pyodide.js` is the check
# that this file still says what it means.
*.js text eol=lf
*.cjs text eol=lf
*.mjs text eol=lf
*.py text eol=lf
*.md text eol=lf

# Vendored Pyodide is published byte-for-byte on jsdelivr. core.autocrlf=true
# would rewrite pyodide.js / pyodide.asm.js / pyodide-lock.json on a Windows
# checkout, which (a) breaks the sha256 verification in
# scripts/test_pyodide_vendor.py and (b) means the vendored bytes are not the
# upstream bytes. Mark them binary so they land exactly as downloaded.
*.wasm binary
assets/vendor/** binary
# `-text` repeated rather than relying on the `binary` macro: a macro only sets
# what it names, and `eol` is still lf from the *.js rule above, so a stale
# attribute could pair with it. Spelling out the three attributes makes the
# intent checkable with check-attr and immune to rule reordering.
assets/vendor/**/*.js -text -diff -merge
assets/vendor/**/*.json -text -diff -merge
